      "title": "Hardware setup starts with mounting discipline",
      "date": "2026-07-20",
      "category": "Hardware / Builds",
      "slug": "hardware-mounting-discipline",
      "summary": "A quick hardware note on why shaker mounting, isolation, gain staging, and physical routing matter before software tuning starts.",
      "tags": [
        "setup",
        "hardware",
        "shakers"
      ],
      "body": "\n        <p>Software tuning cannot fully compensate for poor shaker mounting, loose hardware, or unclear physical routing.</p>\n        <p>Before tuning MCP4SH, the practical baseline is:</p>\n        <ul>\n        <li>hard-mounted shakers or pucks, not floating loosely on the rig,</li>\n        <li>reasonable isolation to reduce frame-wide cross-talk,</li>\n        <li>known left/right/front/rear channel mapping,</li>\n        <li>gain staging that avoids amp clipping or constant bottoming out,</li>\n        <li>and a physical layout that matches the profile being generated.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <p>Rule of thumb: if you hear the shaker more than you feel it, something is probably loose, resonating, or mounted in a way that is wasting energy.</p>\n      "
