      "body": "\n        <p>MCP4SH is not the only SimHub-related thing I have been working on.</p>\n        <p>Some of my smaller tools are not trying to replace anything or become huge platforms. They are more about quality of life: making useful information easier to see, easier to manage, or easier to keep track of while driving and testing.</p>\n        <h2>LCD-box</h2>\n        <p><strong>LCD-box</strong> is a SimHub Dash Studio dashboard focused on system information.</p>\n        <p>The idea is simple: while driving, testing, recording, streaming, or tuning a rig, it can be useful to see what the PC is doing without leaving the sim or opening extra monitoring windows.</p>\n        <p>Depending on the available SimHub data and system setup, it can be used to show things like:</p>\n        <ul>\n        <li>CPU load</li>\n        <li>GPU load</li>\n        <li>memory use</li>\n        <li>temperatures</li>\n        <li>clock / performance information</li>\n        <li>general system status</li>\n        <li>other useful PC or runtime information exposed through SimHub</li>\n        </ul>\n        <p>The goal is not to overload the screen with every possible number. The goal is a clear, compact dashboard for the practical information you may want nearby while testing or driving.</p>\n        <p>In that sense, LCD-box sits in the same general design family as my other tools: less hunting around, more useful information where you need it.</p>\n        <h2>Final Drive Lap Board</h2>\n        <p><strong>Final Drive Lap Board</strong> is a SimHub-related lap board inspired by the idea of a Top Gear-style leaderboard.</p>\n        <p>It is meant as a simple way to keep track of laps, cars, tracks, drivers, attempts, and conditions in a more readable way.</p>\n        <p>The basic idea is: you drive laps, decide what counts, and keep a board.</p>\n        <p>It is not meant to be a strict esports timing system or a replacement for official leaderboard services. It is more of a personal/community lap board tool for testing, comparing, and having fun with different cars and tracks.</p>\n        <p>Final Drive Lap Board is built around things like:</p>\n        <ul>\n        <li>track name</li>\n        <li>car name</li>\n        <li>player / driver name</li>\n        <li>lap times</li>\n        <li>lap attempts</li>\n        <li>custom rule sets</li>\n        <li>special conditions such as wet, fog, snow, or other notes</li>\n        <li>manual control over which laps are stored or counted</li>\n        <li>exportable data / CSV-style records</li>\n        </ul>\n        <p>The manual side is intentional. Not every game exposes reliable invalid-lap detection, and not every session should be judged by the same rules. Sometimes you want a clean official-style board. Sometimes you want a custom challenge. Sometimes you just want to record a lap that was interesting.</p>\n        <p>Final Drive Lap Board gives you a way to do that without pretending every sim reports the same kind of timing data in the same way.</p>\n        <h2>Shared idea</h2>\n        <p>LCD-box, Final Drive Lap Board, and MCP4SH are different tools, but they come from a similar place.</p>\n        <p>They are all about taking something that can be messy, hidden, awkward, or overly technical and turning it into something more useful:</p>\n        <ul>\n        <li>MCP4SH turns telemetry into clearer haptics.</li>\n        <li>LCD-box turns system data into a readable dashboard.</li>\n        <li>Final Drive Lap Board turns lap attempts into a usable board.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <p>Different tools, same general philosophy: make the useful information easier to read, easier to use, and easier to act on.</p>\n      "
